Millimeter-wave (mmWave) radar has emerged as a compelling modality for privacy-preserving Human Pose Estimation (HPE). However, a fundamental disconnect persists: while real-world domestic environments are inherently multi-user and cluttered, existing multi-user systems remain predicated on idealized, open spaces. Through a rigorous empirical study, we expose a critical vulnerability in multi-person scenarios: state-of-the-art methods suffer catastrophic degradation, with the Mean Per Joint Position Error (MPJPE) spiking to 17.4 cm. Given that this magnitude approaches the length of a human forearm, such errors inevitably lead to severe skeletal distortion and limb misalignment, rendering the estimated poses structurally incoherent. This scalability barrier stems from three fundamental physical conflicts: inter-person resolution bin collisions, severe multipath ghosting, and complex occlusions. To break this barrier, we present MuPose, the first mmWave system tailored for robust multi-person HPE in the wild. By integrating advanced signal processing with a deep learning backend, MuPose introduces a hybrid architecture tackling signal chaos via three innovations: signal-level Null Steering to resolve bin collisions at the source, AoA-AoD reciprocity to purge multipath ghosts, and a masked Spatio-Temporal Transformer to infer occluded joints. Extensive evaluations with 23 subjects across 7 complex environments demonstrate that MuPose achieves an MPJPE of 6.7 cm, significantly outperforming SOTA baselines (an MPJPE of 17.4 cm) and enabling precise, ubiquitous sensing in domestic settings.